# What Happened?

Bob ate **pizza** — but described it in deliberately pretentious, deconstructed language to make it sound like a fancy health food.

## The Translation

| Bob's Description | What It Actually Is |
|---|---|
| "Aged organic milk" | Cheese |
| "Seasoned tomato purée" | Pizza sauce |
| "Baked whole wheat" | The crust |

Alice's "wtf" is the punchline: she expected some elaborate healthy dish based on his description, but the photo revealed it was just... pizza.

## Was It Healthy?

**Mostly no, but with caveats:**

- **Whole wheat crust** — slightly better than refined flour (more fiber, nutrients)
- **Tomato sauce** — actually has some nutritional value (lycopene, vitamins)
- **Cheese** — provides protein and calcium, but also lots of saturated fat and calories

So it's a *marginally* healthier pizza than a standard pepperoni-and-extra-cheese pie, but calling it "eating healthy" is a stretch. Bob was technically telling the truth about the ingredients — he just used flowery language to dress up junk food as a wellness meal.

It's a classic case of **technically honest but misleading framing**, which is why Alice's reaction was "wtf" rather than "liar!" [end of text]


